{
  "rules": [
    "Horizon-dependent bias: forecasts are too high in the near term (≈days 1–15), especially during downswings; apply a small negative level shift early in the path.",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: when the market transitions into a sustained rally, the model lags and becomes too low from mid-to-late horizon (≈days 20–30); add a positive drift term that increases with horizon when recent returns are positive.",
    "Over-smoothing of turning points: the model misses sharp drawdowns (predicts too high) and then fails to catch the subsequent stronger rebound (predicts too low later); increase responsiveness to recent momentum/volatility (e.g., scale adjustments by recent realized vol).",
    "Late-horizon upside truncation: in strong uptrends the model’s late-horizon peaks are systematically below realized highs; allow higher terminal levels when the last 10–15 days show persistent higher highs/higher lows.",
    "Asymmetry: downside moves are overestimated in persistence (model stays elevated vs reality early), while upside moves are underestimated in persistence (model mean-reverts too much later); use asymmetric mean-reversion (faster MR after negative shocks, slower MR after positive shocks)."
  ]
}
